WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.672
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:07.636 --> 00:00:08.760
JUSTIN BECKWITH: All right.

00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:09.885
Well, the music turned off.

00:00:09.885 --> 00:00:11.468
And that guy told
me that I'm supposed

00:00:11.468 --> 00:00:12.580
to come up on stage now.

00:00:12.580 --> 00:00:16.400
So let's have a
talk about security.

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:17.910
Yay!

00:00:17.910 --> 00:00:19.140
Congratulations, everyone.

00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:19.650
You made it.

00:00:19.650 --> 00:00:22.050
It's the last day
of the conference.

00:00:22.050 --> 00:00:23.610
When I think about
the kind of topics

00:00:23.610 --> 00:00:26.151
I want to hear about when it's
the last day of a conference--

00:00:26.151 --> 00:00:27.910
I'm tired, I'm
having a good time.

00:00:27.910 --> 00:00:30.480
I just had lunch, I'm
warm-- full belly.

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:33.379
Security in the cloud is not
exactly the most exciting thing

00:00:33.379 --> 00:00:34.420
in the world to everyone.

00:00:34.420 --> 00:00:35.545
So I want to thank you all.

00:00:35.545 --> 00:00:38.022
I'll do my best not
to put you to sleep.

00:00:38.022 --> 00:00:39.480
We're going to have
some fun today.

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:41.188
We're going to look
at a couple of demos,

00:00:41.188 --> 00:00:43.752
talk about some problems,
and hopefully make

00:00:43.752 --> 00:00:45.960
some bad decisions along
the way because that's where

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:49.350
we actually learn our lessons.

00:00:49.350 --> 00:00:51.930
Before we get all into that,
my name is Justin Beckwith.

00:00:51.930 --> 00:00:53.712
I'm an engineering
manager at Google.

00:00:53.712 --> 00:00:55.920
A lot of the demos that
we're going to look at today,

00:00:55.920 --> 00:00:58.172
you're going to be
able to find on GitHub.

00:00:58.172 --> 00:01:00.630
If you want to reach out to me
or get into contact with any

00:01:00.630 --> 00:01:03.254
of the teams that make the cool
products that I'm going to talk

00:01:03.254 --> 00:01:06.390
about today, then all of my
contact information is there.

00:01:06.390 --> 00:01:08.400
Feel free to reach out.

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:10.910
For those of us in the
information security field,

00:01:10.910 --> 00:01:13.230
this has been kind
of a rough year.

00:01:13.230 --> 00:01:15.220
It's been a hard year to watch.

00:01:15.220 --> 00:01:18.130
We've seen multiple
high-profile breaches.

00:01:18.130 --> 00:01:19.590
I'm going to not
do those companies

00:01:19.590 --> 00:01:21.730
the disservice of
putting their logos here

00:01:21.730 --> 00:01:23.250
or any of the news clippings.

00:01:23.250 --> 00:01:25.290
But we've seen several
incidents where

00:01:25.290 --> 00:01:28.500
companies were careless
with their customers' data.

00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:31.230
This is a real problem.

00:01:31.230 --> 00:01:32.730
And the worst part
is it's something

00:01:32.730 --> 00:01:35.599
that we tend to think as
engineers, or as IT admins,

00:01:35.599 --> 00:01:37.140
or [? Cis ?] admins,
we tend to think

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:39.792
that it's a problem that we
can just solve with technology.

00:01:39.792 --> 00:01:41.250
There's a specific
product that can

00:01:41.250 --> 00:01:42.570
help us lock it down this way.

00:01:42.570 --> 00:01:44.754
There's a specific thing
that we can use here.

00:01:44.754 --> 00:01:46.170
While, in some
cases, that's true,

00:01:46.170 --> 00:01:49.530
the truth is this is really
a consumer trust problem.

00:01:49.530 --> 00:01:51.771
Because the moment that we
take on a customer's data,

00:01:51.771 --> 00:01:53.520
and they've made the
relationship with us,

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:56.130
they've trusted us with that
data, and then we lose it,

00:01:56.130 --> 00:01:58.920
we have a breach, that
relationship, oftentimes,

00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:00.420
is impossible to repair.

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:02.490
It's not something that
we, as business owners,

00:02:02.490 --> 00:02:04.260
can ever get back.

00:02:04.260 --> 00:02:06.390
The impact globally,
according to a recent report

00:02:06.390 --> 00:02:10.040
of an average data breach,
is about $4 million.

00:02:10.040 --> 00:02:13.520
Now this is average, going
anywhere from a small security

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:16.020
breach-- which no one likes to
think any of them are a small

00:02:16.020 --> 00:02:16.562
deal--

00:02:16.562 --> 00:02:19.020
all the way up to something
like what happened with Equifax

00:02:19.020 --> 00:02:22.076
where the effects are going
to ripple for quite some time.

00:02:22.076 --> 00:02:24.450
And that's not anything that
any of us want on our watch.

00:02:24.450 --> 00:02:27.630
It's not something anybody wants
to expose their customers to.

00:02:27.630 --> 00:02:29.640
The truth is,
security, information,

00:02:29.640 --> 00:02:32.070
security in the
Cloud with user data,

00:02:32.070 --> 00:02:33.780
this is a business problem.

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:37.410
Once that trust is lost, it's
not coming back, not easily.

00:02:37.410 --> 00:02:39.600
We need 10 positive
interactions to make up

00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:41.072
for one negative interaction.

00:02:41.072 --> 00:02:42.780
And if you loose
someone's customer data,

00:02:42.780 --> 00:02:46.230
and they lose their
social security number

00:02:46.230 --> 00:02:48.090
and have identity
fraud, then, well, it's

00:02:48.090 --> 00:02:51.360
going to take maybe more
than 10 good interactions.

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:52.350
But good news!

00:02:52.350 --> 00:02:54.660
There are no magic bullets.

00:02:54.660 --> 00:02:56.960
There's not one thing that's
going to fix this for us.

00:02:56.960 --> 00:02:58.376
And if anyone's
trying to sell you

00:02:58.376 --> 00:03:00.780
one thing that's going to
fix security in the cloud,

00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:02.760
you probably shouldn't
buy from them.

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:05.730
Nothing is going to
make you 100% secure.

00:03:05.730 --> 00:03:09.330
But what we can do is we can
identify the most common risk

00:03:09.330 --> 00:03:11.670
factors, the highest
risk factors,

00:03:11.670 --> 00:03:13.500
and we can reduce that risk.

00:03:13.500 --> 00:03:16.290
We can mitigate the
highest risk factors.

00:03:16.290 --> 00:03:19.100
And that's largely what we're
going to talk about today.

00:03:19.100 --> 00:03:21.600
We're going to be taking a look
at preventing and mitigating

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:23.400
known vulnerabilities
in our software

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:26.209
stack through containers,
through virtual machines,

00:03:26.209 --> 00:03:28.500
through the software that
we're actually running inside

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:30.791
of our applications, and find
what are some of the most

00:03:30.791 --> 00:03:32.722
common ways that they
can be compromised,

00:03:32.722 --> 00:03:33.930
and how can we mitigate that?

00:03:33.930 --> 00:03:38.259
How can we reduce the surface
area for a potential attack?

00:03:38.259 --> 00:03:40.050
When I talk about
vulnerability protection,

00:03:40.050 --> 00:03:42.849
I like to think about this
in three different buckets.

00:03:42.849 --> 00:03:45.390
The first place, where not a
lot of people really think about

00:03:45.390 --> 00:03:46.714
is at code time.

00:03:46.714 --> 00:03:48.630
Increasingly, the software
that we're building

00:03:48.630 --> 00:03:50.296
and that our software
teams are building

00:03:50.296 --> 00:03:52.044
are comprised of
third-party components.

00:03:52.044 --> 00:03:54.210
It's not like it used to
be where you would have one

00:03:54.210 --> 00:03:56.610
giant standard library,
you'd build your application,

00:03:56.610 --> 00:03:58.260
you write 100% of
the code, and you

00:03:58.260 --> 00:03:59.490
know exactly what's in there.

00:03:59.490 --> 00:04:01.140
How many of us here
are using something

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:08.340
like MPM, Maven, PIP, GoGet,
any GoGet packages, Composer?

00:04:08.340 --> 00:04:10.800
There's one of these--
every language ecosystem has

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:13.290
a third-party ecosystem
that, increasingly,

00:04:13.290 --> 00:04:15.999
has become critical to the
success of our software.

00:04:15.999 --> 00:04:18.540
So for all of those people that
raised hands, how many of you

00:04:18.540 --> 00:04:20.700
read over every line of
code that you're importing

00:04:20.700 --> 00:04:23.280
into your application?

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:24.594
That's amazing!

00:04:24.594 --> 00:04:25.260
I want to talk--

00:04:25.260 --> 00:04:27.660
I want to know
how to scale that.

00:04:27.660 --> 00:04:29.040
We need tools.

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:31.122
We need better tools
to tell us, yes, we

00:04:31.122 --> 00:04:33.080
want to enable our
developers to be successful,

00:04:33.080 --> 00:04:34.590
and to go build great
things, and use what's

00:04:34.590 --> 00:04:35.880
out there in the community.

00:04:35.880 --> 00:04:37.920
At the same time,
we need to make sure

00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:40.230
that there are no known
vulnerabilities, and that,

00:04:40.230 --> 00:04:41.430
honestly, in a lot
of cases, that we

00:04:41.430 --> 00:04:42.990
have the appropriate
licenses in place.

00:04:42.990 --> 00:04:44.906
Because it becomes, not
just a security issue,

00:04:44.906 --> 00:04:47.040
but a compliance issue as well.

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:50.640
The next phase of vulnerability
prevention comes at build time.

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:52.974
And this is where, if you're
containerized organization,

00:04:52.974 --> 00:04:55.098
there are a lot of great
options for you out there.

00:04:55.098 --> 00:04:56.580
Because we've
started to take what

00:04:56.580 --> 00:04:58.038
used to be purely
at the deployment

00:04:58.038 --> 00:04:59.710
time and the
infrastructure stage,

00:04:59.710 --> 00:05:01.680
and we can now pull
that all the way back

00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:03.660
into our build-time processes.

00:05:03.660 --> 00:05:05.650
I want to know what
operating system are we

00:05:05.650 --> 00:05:09.090
using, which distribution,
which version, what packages

00:05:09.090 --> 00:05:09.879
are installed?

00:05:09.879 --> 00:05:11.670
Can I compare those
packages against a list

00:05:11.670 --> 00:05:13.014
of known vulnerabilities?

00:05:13.014 --> 00:05:15.180
And then lastly, once I
have all of that information

00:05:15.180 --> 00:05:17.620
about the build, can I
give it, in my CI system,

00:05:17.620 --> 00:05:21.744
the good old thumbs up or
the-- wah, wah-- thumbs down.

00:05:21.744 --> 00:05:23.910
And that brings me to the
last part of vulnerability

00:05:23.910 --> 00:05:25.747
prevention-- deployment time.

00:05:25.747 --> 00:05:27.330
So you've built your
container, you've

00:05:27.330 --> 00:05:29.250
built your application
binary, you're

00:05:29.250 --> 00:05:31.230
ready to send it
into production.

00:05:31.230 --> 00:05:33.300
What are we doing at
prod time to make sure

00:05:33.300 --> 00:05:35.340
that it's done all that
other checks and all

00:05:35.340 --> 00:05:37.470
those other things
we said it would do?

00:05:37.470 --> 00:05:40.150
How do I know that there
are no third-party packages

00:05:40.150 --> 00:05:40.980
or vulnerabilities?

00:05:40.980 --> 00:05:42.720
How do I know that
we did a scan?

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:45.060
How do I know that my build
system is the one that

00:05:45.060 --> 00:05:46.620
produced that build?

00:05:46.620 --> 00:05:48.840
How do I know this isn't
just an arbitrary binary

00:05:48.840 --> 00:05:51.300
that one of my developers made
on their local machine who

00:05:51.300 --> 00:05:55.190
possibly is disgruntled, or they
had an attack on their desktop?

00:05:55.190 --> 00:05:57.690
These are all questions we need
to think about at each phase

00:05:57.690 --> 00:06:00.210
through our software
development lifecycle process

00:06:00.210 --> 00:06:04.824
to be able to reduce that
surface area of attacks.

00:06:04.824 --> 00:06:06.990
Today, we're largely going
to be talking about build

00:06:06.990 --> 00:06:09.600
time and deployment
time as it relates

00:06:09.600 --> 00:06:13.140
to containers and Kubernetes.

00:06:13.140 --> 00:06:16.620
So lastly, security-- it's
easy to say, oh, this company

00:06:16.620 --> 00:06:18.160
had a huge breach.

00:06:18.160 --> 00:06:20.160
They were negligent;
individuals were negligent.

00:06:20.160 --> 00:06:21.660
It tends not to be
a people problem.

00:06:21.660 --> 00:06:24.030
It's not that any one person
wasn't doing their job.

00:06:24.030 --> 00:06:26.520
It tends to be a
process problem.

00:06:26.520 --> 00:06:28.290
We need to ask the
questions, how do

00:06:28.290 --> 00:06:29.890
we know our software is secure?

00:06:29.890 --> 00:06:31.800
How do we know it's
verified in the build?

00:06:31.800 --> 00:06:34.350
And then how do we know if
there are any new ones once it's

00:06:34.350 --> 00:06:35.470
already in production?

00:06:35.470 --> 00:06:38.070
It's not enough just
to stop vulnerabilities

00:06:38.070 --> 00:06:39.370
from getting into production.

00:06:39.370 --> 00:06:41.040
We have to react
after they're there.

00:06:41.040 --> 00:06:42.870
And for each of
these phases, we need

00:06:42.870 --> 00:06:45.660
to have a well-defined process
and a set of tools that helps

00:06:45.660 --> 00:06:47.350
us commit to that process.

00:06:47.350 --> 00:06:49.590
And that's what we're
going to talk about today.

00:06:49.590 --> 00:06:51.990
It's a process problem.

00:06:51.990 --> 00:06:54.340
And I'm going the
wrong direction.

00:06:54.340 --> 00:06:56.220
Let's go this way.

00:06:56.220 --> 00:06:57.300
All right.

00:06:57.300 --> 00:07:01.220
So now, we've done a lot of
talking, a lot of talking.

00:07:01.220 --> 00:07:03.330
I want to show how some
potentially benign-looking

00:07:03.330 --> 00:07:07.140
applications can actually expose
pretty significant holes that

00:07:07.140 --> 00:07:08.460
allow you to take down an app.

00:07:08.460 --> 00:07:10.710
So we're going to talk about
my terrible, horrible, no

00:07:10.710 --> 00:07:12.960
good, very bad day.

00:07:12.960 --> 00:07:14.172
"This is a fictitious story.

00:07:14.172 --> 00:07:15.380
This did not actually happen.

00:07:15.380 --> 00:07:17.671
Any similarity to any production
service living or dead

00:07:17.671 --> 00:07:20.320
is merely coincidental."

00:07:20.320 --> 00:07:20.820
All right.

00:07:20.820 --> 00:07:23.160
So a fairly typical
application architecture

00:07:23.160 --> 00:07:25.542
that you might find
in Google Cloud

00:07:25.542 --> 00:07:27.250
is going to be run on
top of Kubernetes--

00:07:27.250 --> 00:07:29.270
Kubernetes Engine in my case.

00:07:29.270 --> 00:07:31.710
I have a front end service
that's individually scalable.

00:07:31.710 --> 00:07:33.780
I have a back end
service that's scalable.

00:07:33.780 --> 00:07:36.240
The two are connected via gRPC.

00:07:36.240 --> 00:07:39.420
We have the Cloud Vision API,
I have Cloud storage for files,

00:07:39.420 --> 00:07:41.790
I have some other
random API here off

00:07:41.790 --> 00:07:43.230
in the distance
that I'm calling.

00:07:43.230 --> 00:07:45.780
But in terms of a typical
business application that's

00:07:45.780 --> 00:07:48.589
being built in a containerized
workflow, this is it.

00:07:48.589 --> 00:07:50.880
And this is the type of app
that we're going to look at

00:07:50.880 --> 00:07:53.880
and how I actually personally
made some pretty bad mistakes

00:07:53.880 --> 00:07:58.190
when I built the app
that we're going to see.

00:07:58.190 --> 00:07:59.720
And of course, my
boss is over there

00:07:59.720 --> 00:08:01.062
saying he totally trusts me.

00:08:01.062 --> 00:08:03.520
He's positive I'm going to
build this mission-critical app,

00:08:03.520 --> 00:08:05.930
and it completely won't have
100 vulnerabilities that'll

00:08:05.930 --> 00:08:07.370
tank the business.

00:08:07.370 --> 00:08:09.080
I appreciate his
confidence in me.

00:08:09.080 --> 00:08:11.360
I don't know if he's
out there or not.

00:08:11.360 --> 00:08:13.610
And so our totally
mission-critical business

00:08:13.610 --> 00:08:16.610
application is going
to be about cats.

00:08:16.610 --> 00:08:17.780
This is actually my cat.

00:08:17.780 --> 00:08:19.520
That's Boo Boo Bad Kitty.

00:08:19.520 --> 00:08:22.787
And can we move
over to the demo?

00:08:22.787 --> 00:08:23.620
It's going to look--

00:08:23.620 --> 00:08:24.980
I actually don't know if it's
the demo because it looks

00:08:24.980 --> 00:08:26.570
exactly the same as the slide.

00:08:26.570 --> 00:08:28.110
That's interesting.

00:08:28.110 --> 00:08:30.660
But I can see my mouse moving
around, so I feel pretty good.

00:08:30.660 --> 00:08:33.118
So what this mission-critical
business application does is,

00:08:33.118 --> 00:08:34.760
it's actually going
out to Reddit.

00:08:34.760 --> 00:08:38.309
It's downloading
images from r/aww,

00:08:38.309 --> 00:08:40.580
which is cute animal pictures
if you've never seen it.

00:08:40.580 --> 00:08:42.621
And then it's categorizing
them into two buckets.

00:08:42.621 --> 00:08:46.160
On the left, we have cats, and
on the right, we have dogs.

00:08:46.160 --> 00:08:49.340
And it's using the Cloud Vision
API to figure that out and sort

00:08:49.340 --> 00:08:50.510
it all together.

00:08:50.510 --> 00:08:53.420
So it looks like the dogs are
out to a bit of an early lead.

00:08:53.420 --> 00:08:55.580
We have the cute dog
with the watermelon.

00:08:55.580 --> 00:08:56.660
There are puppies.

00:08:56.660 --> 00:08:58.160
Cats are falling a litt--

00:08:58.160 --> 00:09:00.020
oh, a literal box of cats.

00:09:00.020 --> 00:09:02.487
That's just going
to count as one.

00:09:02.487 --> 00:09:04.820
Like I said here, this is
very important to the business

00:09:04.820 --> 00:09:06.800
because we're trying to
make people productive.

00:09:06.800 --> 00:09:08.750
I need cute pictures of
animals to stay happy.

00:09:08.750 --> 00:09:10.291
If I'm not happy,
I'm not productive,

00:09:10.291 --> 00:09:12.980
therefore, business critical.

00:09:12.980 --> 00:09:14.312
We have the Leia cat.

00:09:14.312 --> 00:09:15.020
And look at that.

00:09:15.020 --> 00:09:17.744
Dogs win.

00:09:17.744 --> 00:09:18.604
[APPLAUSE]

00:09:18.604 --> 00:09:19.520
That was half hearted.

00:09:19.520 --> 00:09:22.910
Come on, let's hear it for dogs.

00:09:22.910 --> 00:09:25.070
Fun fact-- I wrote this
app three years ago.

00:09:25.070 --> 00:09:26.510
Dogs always win.

00:09:26.510 --> 00:09:28.296
It's the weirdest thing.

00:09:28.296 --> 00:09:30.170
And I actually have
years of data in BigQuery

00:09:30.170 --> 00:09:31.700
to prove it if
anybody's interested.

00:09:31.700 --> 00:09:33.260
[LAUGHTER]

00:09:33.260 --> 00:09:33.830
All right.

00:09:33.830 --> 00:09:34.996
Let's go back to the slides.

00:09:37.570 --> 00:09:41.110
Actually-- so this is a
pretty simple application.

00:09:41.110 --> 00:09:44.620
All of this is really doing,
for the front end container,

00:09:44.620 --> 00:09:47.980
it's using an out-of-the-box
Node.js container that I found

00:09:47.980 --> 00:09:48.936
on Docker Hub.

00:09:48.936 --> 00:09:51.310
I went on Docker Hub, I searched
for one, it looked fine,

00:09:51.310 --> 00:09:52.950
I used it.

00:09:52.950 --> 00:09:54.840
I-- [LAUGHS] Yeah.

00:09:54.840 --> 00:09:55.580
Exactly.

00:09:55.580 --> 00:09:58.690
[LAUGHS] This is
three years ago.

00:09:58.690 --> 00:10:00.190
Give me a break.

