00:11:53.750 --> 00:11:54.650
All right.

00:11:54.650 --> 00:11:59.520
So that's-- can we
go back to the deck?

00:11:59.520 --> 00:12:02.830
So that's not good.

00:12:02.830 --> 00:12:03.990
That is very bad.

00:12:03.990 --> 00:12:05.740
This is not what we
want to have happening

00:12:05.740 --> 00:12:09.250
for any of the applications that
we are responsible for running.

00:12:09.250 --> 00:12:11.830
So how could this
have been avoided?

00:12:11.830 --> 00:12:13.630
To help avoid issues
like this, that's

00:12:13.630 --> 00:12:15.970
why Google is working on
the Container Registry

00:12:15.970 --> 00:12:17.350
Vulnerability Scanner.

00:12:17.350 --> 00:12:18.710
Say that three times fast.

00:12:18.710 --> 00:12:20.418
Container Registry
Vulnerability Scanner.

00:12:20.418 --> 00:12:21.790
I'm not going to try.

00:12:21.790 --> 00:12:24.580
Now the Vulnerability Scanner,
it will detect vulnerabilities

00:12:24.580 --> 00:12:26.230
before deploying to production.

00:12:26.230 --> 00:12:28.390
Today, the types of
vulnerabilities for detecting

00:12:28.390 --> 00:12:31.960
are in operating systems,
operating system packages.

00:12:31.960 --> 00:12:34.750
We have an eye towards the
code and third-party ecosystem

00:12:34.750 --> 00:12:35.920
that we discussed.

00:12:35.920 --> 00:12:37.870
We give you notifications
when we find those

00:12:37.870 --> 00:12:39.411
in a variety of ways
that we're going

00:12:39.411 --> 00:12:40.780
to look through demos of today.

00:12:40.780 --> 00:12:46.420
Supports Debian, Ubuntu, Alpine,
with Red Hat Linux and CentOS

00:12:46.420 --> 00:12:48.795
on the way, integrates with
existing tools and workflows.

00:12:48.795 --> 00:12:51.419
And we're going to see a couple
of examples of [? Bloove ?] how

00:12:51.419 --> 00:12:52.060
we can do that.

00:12:52.060 --> 00:12:53.800
And it's currently
available in alpha.

00:12:53.800 --> 00:12:56.383
And there's actually going to
be a link at the end of the deck

00:12:56.383 --> 00:12:59.110
of how y'all can go sign up and
try it out if you'd like to.

00:12:59.110 --> 00:13:00.490
So enough jibber-jabber.

00:13:00.490 --> 00:13:04.580
Let's go take a look at
the demo machine again.

00:13:04.580 --> 00:13:06.610
All right.

00:13:06.610 --> 00:13:10.480
So we talked about the image
I deployed with a whole bunch

00:13:10.480 --> 00:13:11.670
of vulnerabilities.

00:13:11.670 --> 00:13:14.410
I'm going to go over to the
Google Cloud Developer Console.

00:13:14.410 --> 00:13:16.279
This is my Cloud cats project.

00:13:16.279 --> 00:13:18.070
And what we're going
to see is a little bit

00:13:18.070 --> 00:13:20.940
of interesting new UI that pops
up in the Container Registry

00:13:20.940 --> 00:13:21.670
UI.

00:13:21.670 --> 00:13:23.211
So for those of you
who aren't aware,

00:13:23.211 --> 00:13:26.710
Container Registry is Google's
high-performance, secure place

00:13:26.710 --> 00:13:29.480
to store all of your
Docker container images.

00:13:29.480 --> 00:13:32.140
And that's what I'm
using for this app.

00:13:32.140 --> 00:13:34.090
I'm under the ccweb directory.

00:13:34.090 --> 00:13:37.000
And you can see all of the
images that I-- all of the tags

00:13:37.000 --> 00:13:39.166
that I've pushed-- all of
the [? SHAs ?] I've pushed

00:13:39.166 --> 00:13:40.570
for this particular container.

00:13:40.570 --> 00:13:46.810
And you can see here 623
discovered vulnerabilities.

00:13:46.810 --> 00:13:49.660
That's a big number, but
what does it actually mean?

00:13:49.660 --> 00:13:51.579
And so I'm going to dive
in one level deeper,

00:13:51.579 --> 00:13:52.870
and we're going to take a look.

00:13:52.870 --> 00:13:57.610
Within that, 10 critical,
as rated by the CVSS score--

00:13:57.610 --> 00:14:01.387
100 high, 417 medium, and you
can read the numbers on and on.

00:14:01.387 --> 00:14:03.220
So we're going to give
you a detail for each

00:14:03.220 --> 00:14:06.640
of the vulnerabilities that we
were able to discover inside

00:14:06.640 --> 00:14:08.890
of the container, how
critical it is, if there's

00:14:08.890 --> 00:14:11.650
a fix available, the
package, and then lastly,

00:14:11.650 --> 00:14:15.160
almost most importantly, a
link out to the CVE document

00:14:15.160 --> 00:14:17.500
so that you can read what
happened, how to patch it,

00:14:17.500 --> 00:14:18.959
what is the actual risk.

00:14:18.959 --> 00:14:20.500
So let's take a look
at one of these.

00:14:20.500 --> 00:14:24.960
I'm going to click on good
old 4448, one of my favorites.

00:14:24.960 --> 00:14:28.360
And we can see this is
a bug inside of libxml2.

00:14:28.360 --> 00:14:32.440
It looks like it was vulnerable
in Debian Jessie, which,

00:14:32.440 --> 00:14:34.700
for those of you who
follow along with Debian,

00:14:34.700 --> 00:14:37.027
there's a Jessie, Stretch.

00:14:37.027 --> 00:14:39.610
And then the latest is Buster,
but I don't think it's LTS yet.

00:14:39.610 --> 00:14:41.980
Stretch, I'm pretty
sure, is the latest LTS.

00:14:41.980 --> 00:14:45.010
But Jessie is kind of getting
a little bit long in the tooth.

00:14:45.010 --> 00:14:46.720
So it looks like I
must be running on it

00:14:46.720 --> 00:14:50.200
in just a completely old
version of Debian is probably

00:14:50.200 --> 00:14:52.229
what's going on here.

00:14:52.229 --> 00:14:54.270
But let's take a look at
a few of the other ones.

00:14:54.270 --> 00:14:55.936
When you look through
the package names,

00:14:55.936 --> 00:14:59.080
it's not just Linux,
or it's not just glibc.

00:14:59.080 --> 00:15:02.749
ImageMagick-- my app
doesn't use ImageMagick.

00:15:02.749 --> 00:15:05.290
Why am I getting a vulnerability
in ImageMagick for something

00:15:05.290 --> 00:15:06.446
I don't even use?

00:15:06.446 --> 00:15:07.820
And the more you
go through here,

00:15:07.820 --> 00:15:10.015
the more you start to find this.

00:15:10.015 --> 00:15:11.140
So I'm going to go through.

00:15:11.140 --> 00:15:14.096
I'll find-- OpenSSH--

00:15:14.096 --> 00:15:15.220
Well, I probably need that.

00:15:15.220 --> 00:15:18.090
SqLite-- I'm definitely
not using SqLite.

00:15:18.090 --> 00:15:21.040
It's a libvpx-- don't
know what that does.

00:15:21.040 --> 00:15:22.475
Might be important.

00:15:22.475 --> 00:15:24.100
And so what starts
to become very clear

00:15:24.100 --> 00:15:25.815
is that I've got a
collection of problems

00:15:25.815 --> 00:15:27.190
that are starting
to pile up here

00:15:27.190 --> 00:15:30.170
that are leading to this
huge surface area of attack.

00:15:30.170 --> 00:15:36.184
So if we go back to the deck,
how do we actually go and fix

00:15:36.184 --> 00:15:37.100
all of these problems?

00:15:37.100 --> 00:15:39.200
I mean, 623 vulnerabilities.

00:15:39.200 --> 00:15:41.270
You can't just take
them one by one.

00:15:41.270 --> 00:15:43.430
It turns out that
most of these fixes

00:15:43.430 --> 00:15:46.410
fall into a set of
three common buckets.

00:15:46.410 --> 00:15:50.270
The first bucket
is install patches.

00:15:50.270 --> 00:15:52.640
Easier said than done,
but this is something

00:15:52.640 --> 00:15:53.840
that you need to do often.

00:15:53.840 --> 00:15:56.450
You have to do it daily in
your docker build files.

00:15:56.450 --> 00:15:59.240
Add the good old app-get update
and app-get upgrade commands,

00:15:59.240 --> 00:16:03.470
or for whatever distribution
you use and love most.

00:16:03.470 --> 00:16:04.040
Update them.

00:16:04.040 --> 00:16:04.820
Apply patches.

00:16:04.820 --> 00:16:07.500
One of the great things
about the Kubernetes world

00:16:07.500 --> 00:16:09.550
is that, it used to be
with virtual machines,

00:16:09.550 --> 00:16:11.870
planning patch times was
actually kind of hard.

00:16:11.870 --> 00:16:14.920
We may have to take the
product out of production.

00:16:14.920 --> 00:16:17.420
Doing them batch over time and
handling all the restarts was

00:16:17.420 --> 00:16:19.927
difficult. With a
containerized infrastructure,

00:16:19.927 --> 00:16:22.010
there are all kinds of
tools built into Kubernetes

00:16:22.010 --> 00:16:24.360
to help us handle this
and to make it easier.

00:16:24.360 --> 00:16:26.190
So that's kind of
the first bucket.

00:16:26.190 --> 00:16:28.220
The second bucket is
removing the packages

00:16:28.220 --> 00:16:29.420
you're not actually using.

00:16:29.420 --> 00:16:32.522
A stock Debian or
Ubuntu operating system

00:16:32.522 --> 00:16:33.980
comes with a mountain
of stuff that

00:16:33.980 --> 00:16:37.340
makes it great for portability.

00:16:37.340 --> 00:16:39.260
Most applications,
most of the time,

00:16:39.260 --> 00:16:41.780
can run on an out-of-the-box
installation of Debian

00:16:41.780 --> 00:16:42.800
or Ubuntu.

00:16:42.800 --> 00:16:45.140
But the cost of
that is that it's

00:16:45.140 --> 00:16:47.420
got a ton of packages
you probably don't

00:16:47.420 --> 00:16:49.297
need for your application.

00:16:49.297 --> 00:16:51.380
And you really have to go
in and ask the question,

00:16:51.380 --> 00:16:54.720
how many of those did you really
need, and can you remove them?

00:16:54.720 --> 00:16:57.090
And lastly, and kind of the
most extreme case, which

00:16:57.090 --> 00:16:58.940
we're going to take
a look at next,

00:16:58.940 --> 00:17:00.625
is moving to a
different distribution.

00:17:00.625 --> 00:17:02.500
Kind of, now that we've
started to understand

00:17:02.500 --> 00:17:05.839
the ramifications of using
these giant operating systems,

00:17:05.839 --> 00:17:09.079
we've started to look at things
like Alpine, Debian Slim--

00:17:09.079 --> 00:17:14.380
smaller [? distros, ?]
less attack surface area.

00:17:14.380 --> 00:17:16.130
So let's give it a
try, and let's actually

00:17:16.130 --> 00:17:17.796
go deploy-- see what
it's like to deploy

00:17:17.796 --> 00:17:20.950
a more secure container image.

00:17:20.950 --> 00:17:21.609
All right.

00:17:21.609 --> 00:17:24.520
So I'm over here in my
Cloud Cats application,

00:17:24.520 --> 00:17:26.470
I'm going to go
into the Web tier.

00:17:26.470 --> 00:17:29.260
And you can see here
I've got a Docker file.

00:17:29.260 --> 00:17:32.470
Nothing too fancy-- as
I mentioned, node 4.

00:17:32.470 --> 00:17:35.410
So let's go ahead, and
let's upgrade this.

00:17:35.410 --> 00:17:40.370
Oh, running vi on stage,
what could possibly go wrong?

00:17:40.370 --> 00:17:41.320
All right.

00:17:41.320 --> 00:17:44.350
So instead of node 4, I happen
to know that 10 is the latest

00:17:44.350 --> 00:17:45.500
version that's available.

00:17:45.500 --> 00:17:46.770
So let's do that.

00:17:46.770 --> 00:17:48.940
And instead of Debian--
the full Debian package--

00:17:48.940 --> 00:17:50.065
I'm also going to tell it--

00:17:50.065 --> 00:17:52.540
I happen to know that there is
an Alpine package out there.

00:17:52.540 --> 00:17:55.130
Alpine is a much
smaller distribution,

00:17:55.130 --> 00:18:00.700
supports a very tiny subset of
packages, but often just works.

00:18:00.700 --> 00:18:01.400
Oops.

00:18:01.400 --> 00:18:03.740
[LAUGHS] See, I got
what I deserved.

00:18:03.740 --> 00:18:05.341
I got fancy.

00:18:05.341 --> 00:18:05.840
All right.

00:18:05.840 --> 00:18:10.486
So just as an example, I'm going
to do a docker build, example,

00:18:10.486 --> 00:18:12.985
build the directory just to
make sure that everything works.

00:18:17.440 --> 00:18:17.950
All right.

00:18:17.950 --> 00:18:19.440
And it looks like it builds.

00:18:19.440 --> 00:18:20.190
And then I'm going to run--

00:18:20.190 --> 00:18:22.023
I have an update script
that does the build,

00:18:22.023 --> 00:18:24.000
pushes it to Container
Registry, and actually

00:18:24.000 --> 00:18:26.090
replaces the image in
my production service.

00:18:26.090 --> 00:18:27.840
Out of sheer laziness
and a lack of desire

00:18:27.840 --> 00:18:29.920
to type out the full
docker-push command,

00:18:29.920 --> 00:18:32.070
I'm just going to
run that Bash script.

00:18:32.070 --> 00:18:33.310
And we're building again.

00:18:33.310 --> 00:18:34.680
We have Cloud cats.

00:18:34.680 --> 00:18:37.530
Most of the layers
and GCR already exist.

00:18:37.530 --> 00:18:38.995
And we're going
off, and we've just

00:18:38.995 --> 00:18:40.120
rolled the whole thing out.

00:18:40.120 --> 00:18:42.240
So now the new
version is rolled out

00:18:42.240 --> 00:18:45.990
and available on
cats.jbeckwith.com.

00:18:45.990 --> 00:18:50.040
Let's go take a look at what's
going on behind the scenes now.

00:18:50.040 --> 00:18:52.770
So we can see we have an
image we just uploaded now.

00:18:52.770 --> 00:18:55.770
First thing to take into account
is, hey, it's done scanning.

00:18:55.770 --> 00:18:57.700
That was pretty fast.

00:18:57.700 --> 00:19:00.630
We went from an image that was
300 megabytes down to an image

00:19:00.630 --> 00:19:02.670
that's 75 megabytes
with the move

00:19:02.670 --> 00:19:04.950
from Debian over to Alpine.

00:19:04.950 --> 00:19:07.470
And most importantly,
I'm not going

00:19:07.470 --> 00:19:10.170
to claim there are no
vulnerabilities, because that

00:19:10.170 --> 00:19:11.730
would be foolhardy.

00:19:11.730 --> 00:19:13.637
There were none found
against the known

00:19:13.637 --> 00:19:15.720
list of vulnerabilities
in that version of Alpine,

00:19:15.720 --> 00:19:18.450
that version of
Node, fully patched.

00:19:18.450 --> 00:19:21.000
So we went from something
that had 623 vulnerabilities

00:19:21.000 --> 00:19:23.302
by paying just a little
bit more attention

00:19:23.302 --> 00:19:25.260
to what we used as the
base of our Docker image

00:19:25.260 --> 00:19:27.593
and making sure it was something
we understood and where

00:19:27.593 --> 00:19:32.861
it came from, we've reduced
the attack surface that much.

00:19:32.861 --> 00:19:33.360
All right.

00:19:33.360 --> 00:19:34.443
Let's go back to the deck.

00:19:37.520 --> 00:19:39.770
So it's all well and
good that we fixed it.

00:19:39.770 --> 00:19:41.650
But this isn't really
a good process.

00:19:41.650 --> 00:19:45.130
Relying on me to go mill
around inside of the Container

00:19:45.130 --> 00:19:47.675
Registry UI, discover that
there are vulnerabilities,

00:19:47.675 --> 00:19:49.300
react to it, and then
just kind of hope

00:19:49.300 --> 00:19:51.190
it doesn't happen again.

00:19:51.190 --> 00:19:53.641
This is not a
strategy for success.

00:19:53.641 --> 00:19:55.390
So the next thing that
we're talking about

00:19:55.390 --> 00:20:00.160
is getting a good CI/CD
process that uses these tools

00:20:00.160 --> 00:20:02.152
and takes them into account.

00:20:02.152 --> 00:20:03.610
Every time that
we're shipping code

00:20:03.610 --> 00:20:04.700
or we're developing
an application,

00:20:04.700 --> 00:20:06.010
we go through this
natural evolution.

00:20:06.010 --> 00:20:07.330
And you'll see slides like this.

00:20:07.330 --> 00:20:09.190
You've probably seen them all
through the week with the Cloud

00:20:09.190 --> 00:20:10.570
Build announcement
and the other things

00:20:10.570 --> 00:20:11.800
that we've been working on.

00:20:11.800 --> 00:20:14.020
We go from code,
we build, we test,

00:20:14.020 --> 00:20:15.440
and then go into production.

00:20:15.440 --> 00:20:17.229
And at each step
along the way, we

00:20:17.229 --> 00:20:19.270
want to be able to make
the right kinds of checks

00:20:19.270 --> 00:20:21.980
to make sure that we're
not making mistakes.

00:20:21.980 --> 00:20:25.160
This is where the container
analysis API that we have,

00:20:25.160 --> 00:20:27.730
which is part of our container
scanning infrastructure,

00:20:27.730 --> 00:20:29.260
really becomes helpful.

00:20:29.260 --> 00:20:32.590
When you do the build, we can
check what vulnerabilities

00:20:32.590 --> 00:20:33.964
are available in this package.

00:20:33.964 --> 00:20:36.130
We can write what's called
an attestation, or a sort

00:20:36.130 --> 00:20:38.710
of statement of support
saying that, during the build,

00:20:38.710 --> 00:20:39.610
we checked.

00:20:39.610 --> 00:20:41.950
I'm giving it a
stamp of approval.

00:20:41.950 --> 00:20:43.420
This has been scanned.

00:20:43.420 --> 00:20:45.350
When we run the
test-- same thing.

00:20:45.350 --> 00:20:47.260
And then lastly,
at deploy time, we

00:20:47.260 --> 00:20:50.104
want to, again,
ask that question.

00:20:50.104 --> 00:20:52.270
It's great that you're
trying to deploy a container.

00:20:52.270 --> 00:20:53.350
Who built it?

00:20:53.350 --> 00:20:55.720
Can you verify that it
was my build system?

00:20:55.720 --> 00:20:57.210
Can the build
system verify there

00:20:57.210 --> 00:20:58.660
were no known vulnerabilities?

00:20:58.660 --> 00:21:00.880
Can it verify that
all the tests passed?

00:21:00.880 --> 00:21:03.370
Can you verify any other
sorts of things that

00:21:03.370 --> 00:21:04.780
are critical for my business?

00:21:04.780 --> 00:21:06.529
Does it have the right licenses?

00:21:06.529 --> 00:21:09.070
What promises can I make, and
what infrastructure is in place

00:21:09.070 --> 00:21:10.930
to help me make
all of those checks

00:21:10.930 --> 00:21:16.280
before I let this thing into my
production Kubernetes cluster?

00:21:16.280 --> 00:21:18.520
So if that's all well and
good, we can now test--

00:21:18.520 --> 00:21:20.590
going on, we have
a better process.

00:21:20.590 --> 00:21:25.409
What do we do when a new
vulnerability is discovered?

00:21:25.409 --> 00:21:27.700
So we have a couple of things
to help make this easier.

00:21:27.700 --> 00:21:31.599
First, we use what's called
the activity stream inside

00:21:31.599 --> 00:21:32.890
of the Cloud Developer Console.

00:21:32.890 --> 00:21:34.147
You'll get a notification.

00:21:34.147 --> 00:21:36.730
And this is, actually, if you're
enrolled inside of the alpha,

00:21:36.730 --> 00:21:37.854
then you'll get this today.

00:21:37.854 --> 00:21:40.090
And every time a new
vulnerability is discovered,

00:21:40.090 --> 00:21:41.570
you can actually click
on that little bell.

00:21:41.570 --> 00:21:43.528
It'll little show a little
alert with a number.

00:21:43.528 --> 00:21:46.150
And you can go in, dig deep,
get information, use the UI

00:21:46.150 --> 00:21:47.505
that we talked about.

00:21:47.505 --> 00:21:48.880
The other thing
you can configure

00:21:48.880 --> 00:21:50.140
are email notifications.

00:21:50.140 --> 00:21:52.930
So I get a daily [? roll-up ?]
that has all of these security

00:21:52.930 --> 00:21:54.340
vulnerabilities that are found.

00:21:54.340 --> 00:21:57.654
I say all of-- traditionally,
I go fix them now.

00:21:57.654 --> 00:21:59.320
And it lets me know
if there's something

00:21:59.320 --> 00:22:01.960
that I need to work on if you
use email as your workflow

00:22:01.960 --> 00:22:03.000
tool.

00:22:03.000 --> 00:22:05.500
The one that we don't talk a
lot about-- which I don't think

00:22:05.500 --> 00:22:07.240
many people actually
know we have-- is

00:22:07.240 --> 00:22:09.320
we have a mobile application.

00:22:09.320 --> 00:22:11.980
There's an Android app
that works fantastic.

00:22:11.980 --> 00:22:15.319
And you can actually configure
that to get push notifications.

00:22:15.319 --> 00:22:17.110
So if someone in your
team pushes something

00:22:17.110 --> 00:22:18.190
that has a
vulnerability, if it got

00:22:18.190 --> 00:22:19.914
past all of these
other controls,

00:22:19.914 --> 00:22:21.580
you can actually get
a push notification

00:22:21.580 --> 00:22:24.520
letting you know you have a
vulnerable image sitting out

00:22:24.520 --> 00:22:26.920
there in production.

00:22:26.920 --> 00:22:28.900
And then lastly, like
everything that we

00:22:28.900 --> 00:22:32.860
release from Google Cloud,
there is a fully-featured API.

00:22:32.860 --> 00:22:34.660
Now, the fully-featured
API lets you do

00:22:34.660 --> 00:22:36.130
your typical CRUD operations.

00:22:36.130 --> 00:22:38.860
You can create new
occurrences, create nodes,

00:22:38.860 --> 00:22:39.997
read all the information.

00:22:39.997 --> 00:22:41.080
You can do that on a loop.

00:22:41.080 --> 00:22:42.913
But the one that we're
going to look at next

00:22:42.913 --> 00:22:44.710
is our Pub/Sub API.

00:22:44.710 --> 00:22:46.720
Now, what's cool about
this is, with Pub/Sub,

00:22:46.720 --> 00:22:48.730
we'll publish a new
event every time

00:22:48.730 --> 00:22:50.022
a new vulnerability is found.

00:22:50.022 --> 00:22:51.730
And then you can use
that to integrate it

00:22:51.730 --> 00:22:54.740
into any workflow, any
tool, that you want to use.

00:22:54.740 --> 00:22:57.130
Let's take a look at how
I actually did this over

00:22:57.130 --> 00:22:59.770
on the desktop.

00:22:59.770 --> 00:23:02.170
All right.

00:23:02.170 --> 00:23:08.500
So I'm going to go over
to Cloud Functions.

00:23:08.500 --> 00:23:11.110
So Cloud Functions is
an easy-to-use functions

00:23:11.110 --> 00:23:11.985
as a service product.

00:23:11.985 --> 00:23:13.526
And essentially,
the idea is that you

00:23:13.526 --> 00:23:15.250
can have very simple
hooks to execute

00:23:15.250 --> 00:23:17.197
small, discrete pieces of code.

00:23:17.197 --> 00:23:19.030
They've got a couple
of really cool launches

00:23:19.030 --> 00:23:21.310
this week that you should
probably go check out.

00:23:21.310 --> 00:23:23.890
One of the features of
it that I like the most

00:23:23.890 --> 00:23:27.190
is that you can directly trigger
an event based off of something

00:23:27.190 --> 00:23:28.402
coming into Pub/Sub.

00:23:28.402 --> 00:23:29.860
And when you come
over here, you'll

00:23:29.860 --> 00:23:32.230
see all of the
potential trigger type.

00:23:32.230 --> 00:23:34.360
Instead of an HTTP
end point, I'm

00:23:34.360 --> 00:23:36.300
going to look for a
Cloud Pub/Sub end point.

00:23:36.300 --> 00:23:38.800
And then it'll just list all
of the topics that have already

00:23:38.800 --> 00:23:39.521
been created.

00:23:39.521 --> 00:23:41.020
Since we're in alpha
right now, it's

00:23:41.020 --> 00:23:45.380
called resource notes
occurrences v1alpha1.

00:23:45.380 --> 00:23:47.800
So yeah, I expect that you
will all remember that and be

00:23:47.800 --> 00:23:49.910
able to go type it after this.

00:23:49.910 --> 00:23:52.072
But it gives you
the dropdown list.

00:23:52.072 --> 00:23:54.280
And what's cool is, now that
I have those events, now

00:23:54.280 --> 00:23:58.000
every time that we get a new
vulnerability discovered,

00:23:58.000 --> 00:24:01.630
I can go into here, write some
JavaScript, and react to it.

00:24:01.630 --> 00:24:04.952
I'm not going to make you suffer
me writing JavaScript on stage.

00:24:04.952 --> 00:24:06.910
There are other talks
you can watch me do that,

00:24:06.910 --> 00:24:08.590
and people suffer through it.

00:24:08.590 --> 00:24:10.720
But I've already
created a trigger that

00:24:10.720 --> 00:24:13.180
actually goes and does this.

00:24:13.180 --> 00:24:13.960
Let's take a peek.

00:24:16.980 --> 00:24:19.260
So what I decided that
I wanted to see happen

00:24:19.260 --> 00:24:20.720
is I have this code in a--

00:24:20.720 --> 00:24:23.444
it happens to be in a
public GitHub repository.

00:24:23.444 --> 00:24:25.110
Don't post your
security vulnerabilities

00:24:25.110 --> 00:24:26.530
to a public GitHub repository.

00:24:26.530 --> 00:24:29.430
This is just for
demo purposes only.

00:24:29.430 --> 00:24:32.550
But what I'm doing is I'm going
to use the Octokit, the GitHub

00:24:32.550 --> 00:24:33.750
API.

00:24:33.750 --> 00:24:36.090
Every time there's
[? experts.newVuln ?]

00:24:36.090 --> 00:24:38.160
Every time there's
a new vulnerability,

00:24:38.160 --> 00:24:39.960
I'm going to
authenticate to GitHub.

00:24:39.960 --> 00:24:42.690
I'm going to grab the
message that comes out,

00:24:42.690 --> 00:24:45.290
and I'm going to go create
a new GitHub message.

00:24:45.290 --> 00:24:47.910
So it'll just say "new
vulnerability found."

00:24:47.910 --> 00:24:49.800
Let's see if it worked.

00:25:01.161 --> 00:25:01.660
All right.

00:25:01.660 --> 00:25:03.420
So here's the GitHub repository.

00:25:03.420 --> 00:25:06.480
If you too want to be able
to put dogs against cats--

00:25:06.480 --> 00:25:07.900
here it all is.

00:25:07.900 --> 00:25:09.590
And we look at
(LAUGHING) issues.

00:25:09.590 --> 00:25:10.090
OK.

00:25:10.090 --> 00:25:12.390
I have 204 issues,
probably stemming

00:25:12.390 --> 00:25:15.030
from some of the previous
images I had pushed.

00:25:15.030 --> 00:25:18.720
You click in, and it'll give
you some of the API information.

00:25:18.720 --> 00:25:22.330
From here, you could use this to
use the read API to go get all

00:25:22.330 --> 00:25:23.800
of the information you want.

00:25:23.800 --> 00:25:25.967
So if you wanted to integrate
with your existing bug

00:25:25.967 --> 00:25:28.175
system, if you wanted to
integrate with your existing

00:25:28.175 --> 00:25:30.690
alerting system, if you had
special things that you had set

00:25:30.690 --> 00:25:32.490
up that you wanted to
use with Stackdriver,

00:25:32.490 --> 00:25:35.550
you can use this API and
Pub/Sub and Cloud Functions

00:25:35.550 --> 00:25:38.370
to easily kind of
glue it all together.

00:25:38.370 --> 00:25:40.330
Let's go back to the deck.

00:25:40.330 --> 00:25:43.110
So I'm taking this this time.

00:25:43.110 --> 00:25:44.070
All right.

00:25:44.070 --> 00:25:46.020
So that-- I think
that was the Reacting

00:25:46.020 --> 00:25:48.120
to New Vulnerabilities demo.

00:25:48.120 --> 00:25:51.570
So lastly, you can find the
vulnerability information

00:25:51.570 --> 00:25:53.310
inside the developer console.

00:25:53.310 --> 00:25:55.560
We talked about using the
mobile application and push

00:25:55.560 --> 00:25:56.790
notifications.

00:25:56.790 --> 00:25:59.460
We talked about customizable
API-based workflows

00:25:59.460 --> 00:26:01.950
with Pub/Sub, and about
Cloud Functions just,

00:26:01.950 --> 00:26:04.810
in general, being really good
glue for these sorts of things,

00:26:04.810 --> 00:26:07.796
especially when
Pub/Sub is involved.

00:26:07.796 --> 00:26:09.420
Now, you may be
thinking, this is great

00:26:09.420 --> 00:26:10.565
if you're a GCP customer.

00:26:10.565 --> 00:26:11.940
But what if you're
just running--

00:26:11.940 --> 00:26:14.760
if you have Kubernetes, and
you're running it on premise?

00:26:14.760 --> 00:26:17.010
Or what if you have
Kubernetes, and you're running

00:26:17.010 --> 00:26:20.040
this on another cloud provider?

00:26:20.040 --> 00:26:22.710
Well, what about open source?

00:26:22.710 --> 00:26:23.544
That's great.

00:26:23.544 --> 00:26:25.710
One of the projects that
we have that we have worked

00:26:25.710 --> 00:26:28.080
hand-in-hand with just a
whole bunch of other companies

00:26:28.080 --> 00:26:30.170
on defining is called Grafeas.

00:26:30.170 --> 00:26:32.410
And Grafeas-- I'm going
to say the sentence.

00:26:32.410 --> 00:26:34.714
"An open artifact metadata
API to audit and govern

00:26:34.714 --> 00:26:35.880
your software supply chain."

00:26:35.880 --> 00:26:37.930
What the hell does that mean?

00:26:37.930 --> 00:26:39.720
What that actually
means is that we

00:26:39.720 --> 00:26:42.510
have a consistent way
for vendors to tell us

00:26:42.510 --> 00:26:44.790
what artifacts do we
have in our build system

00:26:44.790 --> 00:26:48.060
and running in production, what
vulnerabilities are out there,

00:26:48.060 --> 00:26:50.650
and what auditability
do we need to have.

00:26:50.650 --> 00:26:53.370
And so you probably don't
just have Google Cloud

00:26:53.370 --> 00:26:54.562
scanning your containers.

00:26:54.562 --> 00:26:55.770
You might have Aqua Security.

00:26:55.770 --> 00:26:56.853
You might have Black Duck.

00:26:56.853 --> 00:26:57.931
You might have Twistlock.

00:26:57.931 --> 00:26:59.430
You might have any
number of vendors

00:26:59.430 --> 00:27:01.980
that are going and verifying
different pieces of information

00:27:01.980 --> 00:27:03.240
about your artifacts.

00:27:03.240 --> 00:27:06.060
This is an open
API designed to be

00:27:06.060 --> 00:27:07.530
able to store that information.

00:27:07.530 --> 00:27:09.113
And there's a reference
implementation

00:27:09.113 --> 00:27:11.640
available on GitHub.

00:27:11.640 --> 00:27:14.070
So the metadata types
and providers, they grow

00:27:14.070 --> 00:27:14.850
and they change.

00:27:14.850 --> 00:27:17.310
As we pick up new companies
and we work with them

00:27:17.310 --> 00:27:20.060
and they ask to store
different types of information,

00:27:20.060 --> 00:27:22.050
the standard has
been moving quickly.

00:27:22.050 --> 00:27:25.357
We've had to open contributions
from JFrog, Red Hat, IBM, Black

00:27:25.357 --> 00:27:27.690
Duck-- a lot of the companies
that we just talked about.

00:27:27.690 --> 00:27:29.023
This wasn't done just to Google.

00:27:29.023 --> 00:27:31.140
This is something that we
did with the community.

00:27:31.140 --> 00:27:33.150
You can get a nice
bit of visibility,

00:27:33.150 --> 00:27:35.820
both in terms of assets that
are your source code control,

00:27:35.820 --> 00:27:38.730
all the way through the
build system to production.

00:27:38.730 --> 00:27:41.040
And it can be used
both in the Cloud--

00:27:41.040 --> 00:27:43.770
the container analysis API
we provide on Google Cloud

00:27:43.770 --> 00:27:48.060
implements the Grafeas open API.

00:27:48.060 --> 00:27:50.190
So taking that and connecting
it-- so it's great.

00:27:50.190 --> 00:27:53.220
We have this open API, sort
of, database artifact analysis

00:27:53.220 --> 00:27:54.060
thingy.

00:27:54.060 --> 00:27:56.550
How do we connect
that to Kubernetes?

00:27:56.550 --> 00:27:57.930
It would turn out
that Kubernetes

00:27:57.930 --> 00:27:59.971
has this really cool
feature called the Admission

00:27:59.971 --> 00:28:00.840
Controller.

00:28:00.840 --> 00:28:03.660
It essentially sits in
front of any command that's

00:28:03.660 --> 00:28:06.390
going to be run, intercepts
requests, and lets you

00:28:06.390 --> 00:28:07.800
write a little hook of code.

00:28:07.800 --> 00:28:10.520
The basic of it that, yes,
this looks good, or no,

00:28:10.520 --> 00:28:11.610
get out of here.

00:28:11.610 --> 00:28:13.590
Where this is really
great is for-- remember

00:28:13.590 --> 00:28:15.849
how we were talking about
checking to make sure

00:28:15.849 --> 00:28:17.640
that there are no
vulnerabilities, and that

00:28:17.640 --> 00:28:19.272
who built the container?

00:28:19.272 --> 00:28:20.730
The Kubernetes
Admission Controller

00:28:20.730 --> 00:28:24.110
is how we can
actually go do that.

00:28:24.110 --> 00:28:26.610
If you're looking for a tutorial
on how to work with Grafeas

00:28:26.610 --> 00:28:28.710
and how to use that with--

00:28:28.710 --> 00:28:30.507
and how to set up your
own instance of it,

00:28:30.507 --> 00:28:32.340
there's a great tutorial
out there on GitHub

00:28:32.340 --> 00:28:34.980
made by Kelsey Hightower,
who spoke here this morning.

00:28:34.980 --> 00:28:38.562
I highly suggest to go walk
through that and check it out.

00:28:38.562 --> 00:28:40.770
The next thing that we're
going to talk about-- we're

00:28:40.770 --> 00:28:43.250
able to store the data, we
have this Admission Controller,

00:28:43.250 --> 00:28:44.666
but you'd have to
do a lot of glue

00:28:44.666 --> 00:28:46.380
to kind of put the
pieces together.

00:28:46.380 --> 00:28:50.040
We are also producing Kritis,
another open source project

00:28:50.040 --> 00:28:53.940
built alongside Grafeas that
makes it easy to take and check

00:28:53.940 --> 00:28:57.460
assertions before running your
containers inside the cluster.

00:28:57.460 --> 00:28:59.490
So you can test
assertions, plug directly

00:28:59.490 --> 00:29:01.500
into the existing
Admissions Controller,

00:29:01.500 --> 00:29:05.220
check build provenance, ensure
build scanning happened.

00:29:05.220 --> 00:29:07.410
And also, there are just a
lot of neat little things

00:29:07.410 --> 00:29:10.000
that you can do with it that
it's very easy to extend.

00:29:10.000 --> 00:29:13.860
Again, open source, again,
available on GitHub.

00:29:13.860 --> 00:29:16.031
So let's say that we
wanted to take our cluster,

00:29:16.031 --> 00:29:17.280
and we wanted to lock it down.

00:29:17.280 --> 00:29:18.110
Say, OK.

00:29:18.110 --> 00:29:20.340
Justin, he deployed this
thing with Node.js 4;

00:29:20.340 --> 00:29:21.270
it was terrible.

00:29:21.270 --> 00:29:24.752
It had 623 vulnerabilities,
I think, was the number.

00:29:24.752 --> 00:29:27.210
I don't want to let anybody
deploy anything into my cluster

00:29:27.210 --> 00:29:29.160
if it has vulnerabilities.

00:29:29.160 --> 00:29:30.660
And so we can create
a configuration

00:29:30.660 --> 00:29:33.000
like this with Kritis that,
essentially, says this

00:29:33.000 --> 00:29:35.350
is an image security policy.

00:29:35.350 --> 00:29:38.820
There are some images I'm
going to tell you are just OK.

00:29:38.820 --> 00:29:40.740
But other than that,
I want to make sure

00:29:40.740 --> 00:29:43.890
that you do not check
in any containers that

00:29:43.890 --> 00:29:46.770
have a high security
vulnerability in it--

00:29:46.770 --> 00:29:48.000
just will not allow it.

00:29:48.000 --> 00:29:51.990
But except for CVEs 1, 2, and
3, our security team looked at,

00:29:51.990 --> 00:29:54.060
and they said those
ones were fine.

00:29:54.060 --> 00:29:56.760
So I have this tight control
over what I'm allowed

00:29:56.760 --> 00:29:59.910
to push into my registry.

00:29:59.910 --> 00:30:02.090
And so let's actually take
a look at how this works

00:30:02.090 --> 00:30:03.335
and how you can try it today.

00:30:06.260 --> 00:30:09.410
Every time, back to the cat.

00:30:09.410 --> 00:30:10.830
All right.

00:30:10.830 --> 00:30:13.126
So I have another Google
Cloud project over here.

00:30:13.126 --> 00:30:14.750
And we're looking at
some of the images

00:30:14.750 --> 00:30:16.220
I have in Container Registry.

00:30:16.220 --> 00:30:18.620
We're going to dig down, and
I have this rather benign

00:30:18.620 --> 00:30:21.680
looking Java image that has
one critical vulnerability

00:30:21.680 --> 00:30:23.101
inside of it and 31 others.

00:30:23.101 --> 00:30:25.100
Again, the goal here was
to have a cluster where

00:30:25.100 --> 00:30:28.550
I don't let anything like this
with a critical vulnerability

00:30:28.550 --> 00:30:30.235
pass.

00:30:30.235 --> 00:30:32.360
So I'm going to activate
them, and I'm going to use

00:30:32.360 --> 00:30:34.850
activate the Cloud Shell.

00:30:34.850 --> 00:30:36.780
If this is slow, I will
blame it on the Wi-Fi.

00:30:41.870 --> 00:30:45.290
Word dance-- all right.

00:30:45.290 --> 00:30:48.000
So we're going to go into here.

00:30:48.000 --> 00:30:50.210
I'm going to take a
deep look at the image,

00:30:50.210 --> 00:30:51.410
and I need to copy this.

00:30:51.410 --> 00:30:54.020
I'm going to get the full SHA.

00:30:54.020 --> 00:30:55.550
And if we look in
this directory,

00:30:55.550 --> 00:30:59.266
you're going to see
that I have a pod.yaml.

00:30:59.266 --> 00:31:01.390
And this pod.yaml,
all it does is

00:31:01.390 --> 00:31:04.180
says this is the name of
the pod we're going to run.

00:31:04.180 --> 00:31:08.110
Please use this image, which
matches the one that we had

00:31:08.110 --> 00:31:10.842
pushed, and open up port 80.

00:31:10.842 --> 00:31:12.550
So let's go ahead,
and let's try to start

00:31:12.550 --> 00:31:14.310
our pod on the cluster.

00:31:14.310 --> 00:31:21.922
I'm going to do a kubectl
create-f, pod.yaml.

00:31:21.922 --> 00:31:25.070
And pretty quickly, it's
going to try setting that up.

00:31:25.070 --> 00:31:27.730
It's going to take
a look at the image.

00:31:27.730 --> 00:31:29.980
It's going to take an
uncomfortable amount of time,

00:31:29.980 --> 00:31:33.754
and then it's going to reject
my request to create the pod.

00:31:33.754 --> 00:31:35.920
And you can see here, the
error that we're getting--

00:31:35.920 --> 00:31:38.080
"the admission hook,
Kritis validation hook

00:31:38.080 --> 00:31:41.740
denied the request because
it found violations

00:31:41.740 --> 00:31:43.397
based on our policy."

00:31:43.397 --> 00:31:45.730
So now we don't have to worry
about who's deploying what

00:31:45.730 --> 00:31:46.460
into our cluster.

00:31:46.460 --> 00:31:49.720
We can give tight controls
on our cluster, both inside

00:31:49.720 --> 00:31:53.260
of Google Cloud, and in our
own Kubernetes/Grafeas/Kritis

00:31:53.260 --> 00:31:56.830
deployment and lock it down and
ensure that we have a semi--

00:31:56.830 --> 00:31:59.182
a more secure environment.

00:31:59.182 --> 00:32:00.550
Let's go back to the deck.

00:32:03.290 --> 00:32:05.060
So what is the timeline
on all of this?

00:32:05.060 --> 00:32:07.812
I mentioned that we're
currently in alpha.

00:32:07.812 --> 00:32:09.770
All of the features we
saw today are available.

00:32:09.770 --> 00:32:12.210
And you can use them
inside of the alpha group.

00:32:12.210 --> 00:32:13.870
It integrates with
your existing images

00:32:13.870 --> 00:32:14.911
that are pushed into GCR.

00:32:14.911 --> 00:32:16.960
So if you're an existing
Kubernetes or existing

00:32:16.960 --> 00:32:19.600
Container Registry customer,
we can actually turn this on,

00:32:19.600 --> 00:32:21.680
and it all just lights up today.

00:32:21.680 --> 00:32:24.470
To get access, you can join the
Container Analysis Users group.

00:32:24.470 --> 00:32:26.450
And we're going to share the
link for that in a moment.

00:32:26.450 --> 00:32:27.310
The things we're working on.

00:32:27.310 --> 00:32:28.330
What's coming up in the works.

00:32:28.330 --> 00:32:30.746
We had a lot of this we wanted
to get ready for this week.

00:32:30.746 --> 00:32:31.660
It's not far away.

00:32:31.660 --> 00:32:33.280
It's coming very soon.

00:32:33.280 --> 00:32:35.920
The big one for me is
support for Red Hat, Linux,

00:32:35.920 --> 00:32:39.657
and CentOS Cloud Security
Command Center integration--

00:32:39.657 --> 00:32:41.740
so kind of bringing this
into the security control

00:32:41.740 --> 00:32:43.780
stuff that's available
at the organization level

00:32:43.780 --> 00:32:46.330
and plugging in all the
policy notification management

00:32:46.330 --> 00:32:51.490
there and then just general UI
optimizations and bug fixes.

00:32:51.490 --> 00:32:54.075
So to wrap this all
up, security is hard.

00:32:54.075 --> 00:32:56.200
Any of us that have worked
in the field for a while

00:32:56.200 --> 00:32:58.450
know there's one thing
that will fix it.

00:32:58.450 --> 00:33:01.254
There's no silver bullet, but
our businesses depend on it.

00:33:01.254 --> 00:33:03.670
We have tools that are going
to help you get through this.

00:33:03.670 --> 00:33:05.350
We have the right
CI/CD offerings.

00:33:05.350 --> 00:33:06.490
We have the scanner.

00:33:06.490 --> 00:33:08.620
We're doing all of
this in the open,

00:33:08.620 --> 00:33:10.930
and we want to do it with you.

00:33:10.930 --> 00:33:12.680
And lastly, if you
want to join the group,

00:33:12.680 --> 00:33:15.715
please send your questions
to containeranalysi

00:33:15.715 --> 00:33:18.700
sfeedback@google.com.

00:33:18.700 --> 00:33:22.000
One last, last, last thing.

00:33:22.000 --> 00:33:23.800
We actually released
a new site today--

00:33:23.800 --> 00:33:27.550
cloud.google.com
/containers/security that goes

00:33:27.550 --> 00:33:30.070
way more in depth on everything
I talked about today as long

00:33:30.070 --> 00:33:33.370
as a variety of other topics
extending all the way out

00:33:33.370 --> 00:33:36.940
into the VM image, to
network security, to IAM,

00:33:36.940 --> 00:33:39.820
to all of those other pieces
that we need to think about.

00:33:39.820 --> 00:33:41.800
Really recommend folks
to go give this a look

00:33:41.800 --> 00:33:44.770
and send us feedback because
we're really excited about it,

00:33:44.770 --> 00:33:46.390
and thanks.

00:33:46.390 --> 00:33:48.490
[APPLAUSE]

00:33:52.690 --> 00:33:56.040
